WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.370
JENNY BROWN: Before you can
use the power of the Cloud

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:06.330
to serve your content, you
have to get your data into it.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.430
In this episode of
Cloud Storage Bytes,

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.023
we talk all about the
ways you can upload data.

00:00:11.023 --> 00:00:13.065
So buckle up, because
we're heading to the Cloud.

00:00:13.065 --> 00:00:17.568
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.870
When it comes to writing
data into Cloud Storage,

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:25.090
you have a few
different options.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.040
First is the easiest option--

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.930
using the Cloud Console.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.330
This provides you with
an in-browser experience

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:34.270
where you can easily click
to create buckets and folders

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.430
and then choose or
drag and drop the files

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:39.100
from your local
machine to upload.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.590
Second is the command line tool.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:44.980
While the Cloud Console is fine
for easy uploads and simplistic

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.770
serving situations, for
production environments,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.920
you often need an automated
command line solution.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.680
For this, we provide
the gsutil tool.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.260
Gsutil is a Python
application that

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.440
lets you access Cloud Storage
from the command line.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.320
It provides you with the ability
to do all sorts of things

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.500
like creating buckets, moving
objects, or even editing

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.190
metadata.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.860
To use it, simply run
the gsutil program

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.170
with a variety of
command line options.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:14.750
For example, this command
uploads the directory

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.450
of files from your local
machine to your Cloud Storage

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:19.530
bucket using parallel upload.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.890
And this command lists
out specific objects

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.400
that have a version specific
URL using a wild card.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.320
You can see the other
cool stuff this tool can

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.960
do in the documentation
linked below.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.270
Up next is our client libraries.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.150
At some point, you might need
to interface with Cloud Storage

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.700
directly from your code,
rather than going out

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.440
to a command line option.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.930
You can include the client
libraries into your code

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.530
and call a simple API to get
data into a bucket or folder.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:48.780
And regardless of your
language, we've got you covered.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.200
And finally, if none of
that suits your fancy,

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.410
there's always the good
old JSON and XML APIs,

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.990
which can let you
kick off an HTTP post

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:00.680
request to upload data
directly to a bucket or folder.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:04.620
It's a bit more complicated,
but it's there if you need it.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.070
Now, for you folks
with lots of data,

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:08.810
it's worth noting
that it might not

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.880
be feasible to upload
all of that data

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.390
directly from your on-prem
systems to Google Cloud.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.750
For that, you can use the Cloud
Storage Transfer Appliance.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.180
We ship you a fancy
device to your location,

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.970
you can connect it and
transfer at a super high speed,

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:24.800
and then send it back to us.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.220
Plus, we get this cool looking
box on your desk for a while.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.010
And don't worry if your
data is on another Cloud.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.560
We've got easy-to-use
guides to help you get up

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.290
and running with supporting
a multi-Cloud environment,

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:39.830
getting data over to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.837
And of course, now that the
data is in Cloud Storage,

00:02:42.837 --> 00:02:44.420
you've got to figure
out the best ways

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.670
to serve it to your
users worldwide.

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:48.470
But that's for another episode.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.507
Subscribe, give
us a like, and let

00:02:50.507 --> 00:02:52.340
us know what features
of Cloud Storage you'd

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.990
like to learn more about.

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:56.630
Thanks for joining us for this
Quick Byte of Cloud Storage.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:00.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

